WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.710
Madhuka Kumari took out a loan

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.503
from a microfinance company.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.920
She pretty quickly became unable

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.723
to make the $30 monthly
payments on those loans.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.430
And when she fell behind,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.900
she started getting
visits from loan officers,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.880
and they became increasingly aggressive,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.303
demanding their money back.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.870
Ultimately, it led Kumari to
attempt to commit suicide.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:36.107
She poured kerosene over her
head and set herself alight.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.560
Microfinance was the
development world's attempt

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.420
to help do away with poverty
by giving small loans

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.230
to people in the developing world.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.270
In much of the developing world,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.150
people don't have access to capital.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.300
They call them unbanked

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.160
because they can't get bank accounts.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.850
So, it started with this
kind of noble aspiration.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.180
It began as a charitable endeavor

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.423
and it has since morphed into
something quite different.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.140
In many societies, it's
doubly hard for women

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.840
to get access to financial services.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.460
And microfinance was seen as a panacea

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.663
in terms of targeting women in particular.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.630
What we found was a network

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.460
of predatory microfinance
institutions operating

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.540
around the world who were
promising to lift people

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.120
out of poverty and include women

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.040
in the financial services system.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.500
When in reality, what
they were actually doing

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.210
was forcing women to sell
property to repay small loans,

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.750
putting them in prison in some instances,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.490
and in Sri Lanka, more than 200 suicides

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:04.013
have been linked to microfinance loans.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.430
Despite these problems,
it is still being funded

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.970
by public money and publicly
funded development banks.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.350
So, public money that's supposed
to be helping the poor is,

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.413
in some cases, being given
to people who exploit them.

00:02:55.563 --> 00:02:57.940
Modern microfinance traces its roots

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.170
back to the economist, Muhammad Yunus,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.160
who in the 1970s, really
pioneered this form of lending

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.510
in Bangladesh after a
devastating drought there.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.273
With no means of support
and no place to go,

00:03:13.273 --> 00:03:15.570
they're in government camps.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.990
And his innovation was
to lend these people

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:22.080
small sums of money
with low interest rates.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.200
And they would build their
businesses on that investment.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.620
And he found that, actually,
it was very successful,

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.350
and these people repaid their loans.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.883
And a global movement
was born out of that.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.190
I never had any intention
or any plan or any thought

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.053
that I will ever lend money
to become a banker of a sort.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.500
I was looking for opportunity
to do some tiny little thing

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.840
which will be helpful to another person.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.890
And one thing hit me very
hard is the victimization

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.430
of people by the loan sharks.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.750
Giving tiny loans, as
little as $1, $2 loan,

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.910
and grab everything the other person has.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.500
And the poor people are
desperate to find some money

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.850
for survival and so on.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.323
So they had to borrow money.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.640
Seeing this repeatedly in the village,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.490
I was wondering whether I can protect them

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.270
from the loan shark.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:21.120
All people are entrepreneurs.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.490
All they need is money.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.565
Money is the oxygen for entrepreneurship.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.730
Within a couple years, you know,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.460
he won the Nobel Peace Prize.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.150
The UN declared it the Year of microcredit

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.590
because it was the hot idea
in the development world.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:40.890
Poverty is in the system.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.760
So, we sort out the system,
correct those system,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.120
nobody will remain poor.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.370
It's a very simple logic.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.210
I believe that Dr. Yunus is a person

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:55.630
that understands that love is a verb.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:56.963
That love is an action.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.430
This is an important trend

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:05.430
in an era of economic instability
and entrenched poverty.

00:05:05.873 --> 00:05:08.940
The Clinton Global Initiative,
the Gates Foundation,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.040
Matt Damon, Bono, I mean, it
really captured the imagination

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.360
of people because it had
everything that you wanted.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.600
It had profit.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.410
You can make some profit while doing good.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.660
So that's kind of the neoliberal dream.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.030
And it also depended upon, you know,

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.600
people being self-reliant.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.270
And everyone wants to
think that, you know,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.673
that a little entrepreneurial
spirit can help people.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.290
What was particularly
seductive about microfinance

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.840
was that the microfinance institutions

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.320
that were receiving government funding

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.550
would one day transition to profitability

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.400
and have a sustainable business model.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.030
But by making these
institutions profit-seeking,

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.620
there's an inherent conflict there

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.983
between doing social
good and making money.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.380
Banco Compartamos really was the big bang

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.920
of commercial microfinance.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.938
It had been founded by a
group of religious people,

00:06:06.938 --> 00:06:08.240
and it was a charity.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.770
But they wanted to scale up,

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:11.040
and they thought the best way to do that

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.533
was to become commercial.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.670
They had an IPO in 2007 as a small lender

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:23.200
which had come to dominate the
microfinance world in Mexico.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.850
All of a sudden, it had a
valuation worth $2 billion.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.900
Some of the NGOs that had invested in it,

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.810
like ACCION, which is based in the U.S.,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.910
took a million dollar
public money from USAID,

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.060
the U.S. development agency,
invested in Compartamos,

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.820
and after the IPO, boom,

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.963
they had $350 million
worth of value in it.

00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:48.017
It made a lot of people very rich.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.640
But it also, in the financial world

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.970
was like ringing the dinner bell.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:54.830
Because people saw how
much money could be made,

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:56.563
and so, it changed everything.

00:08:08.363 --> 00:08:10.460
I think the big hope was that

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.100
by bringing in the commercializing

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.270
and bringing in, you know, more capital,

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:15.390
you could do more good.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:19.700
But when big money comes in,
it often demands big returns.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:20.890
And what we've found is the world

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.860
kind of turned its attention away from it.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.180
And as that's happened,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.940
the financial world
has continued to do it.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:28.570
And people's tax dollars are underwriting

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:29.643
a huge amount of it.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:35.420
In 2020 alone, there was
more than $50 billion worth

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:38.270
of committed funds from development banks,

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:41.270
commercial lenders,
non-governmental organizations,

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.660
and socially-minded
impact investment firms.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:45.593
That was a record.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.640
Sri Lanka is a country where
the consumer protections

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.800
are very sparse.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.830
And there is a burgeoning
microfinance industry there.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:01.660
Lending ever-larger
amounts of money to poor,

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.360
mostly financially illiterate people

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.963
who are getting stuck in
a vicious cycle of debt.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:14.700
LOLC started out as a
leasing company in Sri Lanka

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.403
with the backing of the
World Bank in the 1980s.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:21.800
It has since morphed

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.170
into one of the world's
biggest microfinance lenders,

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.980
backed by hundreds of millions of dollars

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.380
of financial assistance

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.400
from pretty much every development bank

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.013
that you can think of.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:36.700
For the next generation,

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:38.750
a new dawn has arrived,

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.313
full of promise.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:46.750
Today, LOLC is Sri Lanka's
most profitable listed company.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.420
And its owner is one of
the country's richest men.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.470
And the lion's share
of that money has come

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:56.103
from microfinance: from
lending to poor people.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.020
There's very little regulatory oversight

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.600
of these microfinance institutions.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.060
And so, what you find is
that they are operating

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.350
really on their own terms.

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:12.350
We found multiple instances
of LOLC microfinance companies

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:17.450
using pressure tactics to
force women to repay money

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.940
that were leading these women
into real despair and torment.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.950
Cambodia became the first foreign market

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.970
that LOLC expanded into.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.780
It bought a stake in a
microfinance lender there,

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:31.410
called Prasac,

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.453
which was co-owned by a
bunch of development banks.

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:38.990
Cambodia has become a poster child

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.310
for what can go wrong with microfinance.

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:44.720
It's one of the world's poorest countries,

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.993
and now, one of its most overindebted.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:54.470
In Cambodia, loan sizes have
ballooned over the past decade.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.300
And now, more than one
in five adult Cambodians

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.590
has a microfinance loan.

00:14:11.369 --> 00:14:14.426
Not in my wildest dream did I thought

00:14:14.426 --> 00:14:17.291
our work would be used by someone

00:14:17.291 --> 00:14:20.359
to make themselves loan sharks.

00:14:20.359 --> 00:14:23.867
So that's a very sad part of the story.

00:14:23.867 --> 00:14:25.607
You are driven by self-interest,

00:14:25.607 --> 00:14:28.180
and this is translated as
maximization of profit.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:32.090
So basically, you became
money-making robots.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.370
And you can destroy all
the poor people's lives.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:36.520
You don't care less
because you make money.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.220
When you make the point to many people

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.260
in the development banking world

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.330
that there is little evidence to show

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.640
that microfinance has achieved its aims

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:50.830
of lifting the poor out of poverty,

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:54.310
they often say that it's much
better than the alternative,

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.730
which is that these poor people

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.600
would have to resort to loan sharks.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.760
While that's difficult to dispute,

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:05.010
It really doesn't justify
the hundreds of billions

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.800
that has been put into this industry

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:12.800
with very scant evidence of it
having any long-term impact.

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:17.070
The U.S. government,
the British government,

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.120
the World Bank, Proparco, the French bank,

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.540
they give to different banks.

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.550
And when you ask them,
"Well, why did you give

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.380
to this lender who has a history

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.140
of forcing people to sell their land

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.350
and of throwing people
into debtors' prison?"

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.040
And they'll say, "Oh,
we didn't know that."

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.890
That someone else was supposed to vet it.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:35.750
And ultimately, they
can just pass the buck

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.073
of responsibility onto someone else.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:45.000
No one wants to say what is an
acceptable amount of interest

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.550
and what is an acceptable
amount of profit level

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:52.220
for a lender that says it's a
socially-minded institution.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.840
So, people all just kind
of look the other way.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.900
And I think people don't wanna reckon that

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.440
because there's a lot of people

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.040
making a lot of money off of it.

